 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 4.06 — Jacobi Fields and Comparison\n\n4.05 produced curvature as an algebraic object. This notebook makes it *do* something: curvature controls how neighbouring geodesics drift apart, and that single fact propagates into global statements about the shape of the whole space.\n\n**The Jacobi equation.** Take a one-parameter family of geodesics $\\gamma_s(t)$ and let $J(t) = \\partial_s\\gamma_s(t)|_{s=0}$ be the variation field. Differentiating the geodesic equation in $s$ gives\n\n$$\\frac{D^2 J}{dt^2} + R(J,\\dot\\gamma)\\dot\\gamma = 0.$$\n\nIn an orthonormal frame parallel along $\\gamma$, with $J$ normal to $\\dot\\gamma$ and $|J| = j(t)$, this collapses in **two dimensions** to\n\n$$\\ddot j + K\\,j = 0,$$\n\nas clean a statement as the subject offers. Curvature is the restoring force on geodesic separation.\n\n| $K$ | $j$ with $j(0)=0$, $\\dot j(0)=1$ | geodesics |\n|---|---|---|\n| $K > 0$ | $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt K}\\sin(\\sqrt K t)$ | converge, and **meet** at $t = \\pi/\\sqrt K$ |\n| $K = 0$ | $t$ | separate linearly |\n| $K < 0$ | $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{-K}}\\sinh(\\sqrt{-K}t)$ | separate exponentially |\n\nThis is the same equation as **geodesic deviation** in general relativity: a tidal force is the failure of nearby free-fall trajectories to stay parallel.\n\n**Conjugate points.** If a non-trivial Jacobi field vanishes at $t = 0$ and again at $t = t_0$, the points $\\gamma(0)$ and $\\gamma(t_0)$ are **conjugate**. Two things happen there: the exponential map $\\exp_p$ becomes singular, so normal coordinates break down; and $\\gamma$ **stops minimising** beyond $t_0$. That is the precise version of the caveat at the end of 4.03.\n\n**Comparison theorems.** Curvature *bounds* are enough to force topology:\n\n- **Bonnet–Myers.** If $\\mathrm{Ric} \\ge (n-1)K g$ with $K > 0$ and $M$ is complete, then $\\mathrm{diam}(M) \\le \\pi/\\sqrt{K}$; hence $M$ is compact and $\\pi_1(M)$ is finite.\n- **Cartan–Hadamard.** If $M$ is complete, simply connected and has $K \\le 0$ everywhere, then $\\exp_p: T_pM \\to M$ is a diffeomorphism. There are no conjugate points, and $M$ is diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nThe second is why hyperbolic space, for all its curvature, is topologically as dull as the plane — and why the interesting hyperbolic *manifolds* of 6.04 must be quotients.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\nassume(R0 > 0, u > 0, u < %pi)$\n\n/* --- The scalar Jacobi equation in 2D:  j'' + K j = 0 --- */\njac_sol(Kval) := ode2( 'diff(j,t,2) + Kval*j = 0, j, t)$\nprint(\"solutions of j'' + K j = 0:\")$\nprint(\"   K = 1/R₀² : \", jac_sol(1/R0^2))$\nprint(\"   K = 0     : \", jac_sol(0))$\nprint(\"   K = −1    : \", jac_sol(-1))$\n\n/* the field with j(0)=0, j'(0)=1 — the one that measures the spread of\n   geodesics fanning out from a single point */\nfan(Kval) := block([gen],\n  gen : jac_sol(Kval),\n  ic2(gen, t=0, j=0, 'diff(j,t)=1) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"with j(0) = 0, j'(0) = 1:\")$\nprint(\"   K = 1/R₀² : \", fan(1/R0^2))$\nprint(\"   K = 0     : \", fan(0))$\nprint(\"   K = −1    : \", fan(-1))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"first conjugate point = first positive zero:\")$\nprint(\"   K = 1/R₀² : t = πR₀   (the antipode)\")$\nprint(\"   K = 0, K < 0 : none — sin becomes t, then sinh, neither of which\")$\nprint(\"                  returns to zero.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "solutions of j'' + K j = 0:\n   K = 1/R₀² :  j = %k1*sin(t/R0)+%k2*cos(t/R0)\n   K = 0     :  j = %k2*t+%k1\n   K = −1    :  j = %e^-t*%k2+%e^t*%k1\nwith j(0) = 0, j'(0) = 1:\n   K = 1/R₀² :  j = R0*sin(t/R0)\n   K = 0     :  j = t\n   K = −1    :  j = %e^t/2-%e^-t/2\nfirst conjugate point = first positive zero:\n   K = 1/R₀² : t = πR₀   (the antipode)\n   K = 0, K < 0 : none — sin becomes t, then sinh, neither of which\n                  returns to zero."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- The full vector Jacobi equation, checked on the unit sphere --- */\nts(e) := ratsimp(trigsimp(e))$\nchristoffel_hand(g, vars) := block([ginv, n_],\n  n_ : length(vars),  ginv : ratsimp(invert(g)),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( sum( ginv[k][l]*( diff(g[j][l], vars[i])\n                        + diff(g[i][l], vars[j])\n                        - diff(g[i][j], vars[l]) ), l, 1, n_)/2 ),\n    j,1,n_), i,1,n_), k,1,n_) )$\nriem_hand(g, vars) := block([G, n_],\n  G : christoffel_hand(g, vars), n_ : length(vars),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( diff(G[l][j][k], vars[i]) - diff(G[l][i][k], vars[j])\n        + sum( G[l][i][m]*G[m][j][k] - G[l][j][m]*G[m][i][k], m, 1, n_) ),\n    k,1,n_), j,1,n_), i,1,n_), l,1,n_) )$\n\n/* The family: meridians γ_s(t) = (u = t, v = s).  Each is a geodesic, and\n   J = ∂γ/∂s = ∂_v, so in components J = [0, 1] — constant coefficients,\n   but NOT a parallel field, because the connection is not trivial. */\ng1 : matrix([1,0],[0,sin(u)^2])$\nG1 : christoffel_hand(g1, [u,v])$\nR1 : riem_hand(g1, [u,v])$\nJc : [0, 1]$                     /* J^u, J^v */\nTc : [1, 0]$                     /* γ' = ∂_u */\n\n/* first covariant derivative along γ: (DJ/dt)^k = ∂_t J^k + Γ^k_{im} T^i J^m */\nDJ : makelist( ts( diff(Jc[k], u)\n       + sum(sum( G1[k][i][m]*Tc[i]*Jc[m], m,1,2), i,1,2) ), k, 1, 2)$\nD2J : makelist( ts( diff(DJ[k], u)\n       + sum(sum( G1[k][i][m]*Tc[i]*DJ[m], m,1,2), i,1,2) ), k, 1, 2)$\nRJ  : makelist( ts( sum(sum(sum(\n         R1[k][i][j][l]*Jc[i]*Tc[j]*Tc[l], l,1,2), j,1,2), i,1,2) ), k, 1, 2)$\n\nprint(\"unit sphere, J = ∂v along a meridian:\")$\nprint(\"   DJ/dt   = \", DJ)$\nprint(\"   D²J/dt² = \", D2J)$\nprint(\"   R(J,γ')γ' = \", RJ)$\nprint(\"   D²J/dt² + R(J,γ')γ' = \", ts(D2J + RJ), \"   ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   |J| = √(g(J,J)) = \", ts(sqrt(sum(sum(g1[i][j]*Jc[i]*Jc[j],j,1,2),i,1,2))),\n      \" = sin t, which is the fan solution above with K = 1.\")$\nprint(\"   It vanishes at t = 0 and t = π: the poles are conjugate, and a\")$\nprint(\"   great-circle arc stops minimising once it passes the antipode.\")$\n\n/* --- Surfaces of revolution: f is itself a Jacobi field --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"SURFACE OF REVOLUTION, g = du² + f(u)² dv², K = −f''/f.\")$\ndepends(f, u)$\nprint(\"   Jacobi equation applied to j = f :  f'' + K f = \",\n      ratsimp( diff(f,u,2) + (-diff(f,u,2)/f)*f ))$\nremove(f, dependency)$\nprint(\"   So f is always a Jacobi field along the meridians — it is the\")$\nprint(\"   variation field of the family of meridians themselves.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   sphere      f = sin u  : zeros at 0, π      → conjugate points\")$\nprint(\"   plane       f = r      : zero at 0 only     → none\")$\nprint(\"   hyperbolic  f = sinh u : zero at 0 only     → none\")$\nprint(\"   The presence of conjugate points is legible directly from f.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "unit sphere, J = ∂v along a meridian:\n   DJ/dt   =  [0,cos(u)/sin(u)]\n   D²J/dt² =  [0,-1]\n   R(J,γ')γ' =  [0,1]\n   D²J/dt² + R(J,γ')γ' =  [0,0]    ✓\n   |J| = √(g(J,J)) =  sin(u)\n                      = sin t, which is the fan solution above with K = 1.\n   It vanishes at t = 0 and t = π: the poles are conjugate, and a\n   great-circle arc stops minimising once it passes the antipode.\nSURFACE OF REVOLUTION, g = du² + f(u)² dv², K = −f''/f.\n   Jacobi equation applied to j = f :  f'' + K f =  0\n   So f is always a Jacobi field along the meridians — it is the\n   variation field of the family of meridians themselves.\n   sphere      f = sin u  : zeros at 0, π      → conjugate points\n   plane       f = r      : zero at 0 only     → none\n   hyperbolic  f = sinh u : zero at 0 only     → none\n   The presence of conjugate points is legible directly from f."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Non-constant curvature: the torus of revolution --- */\n/* Tube radius 1, centre radius 2.  With u the arclength along a meridian,\n   g = du² + f(u)² dv²,  f(u) = 2 + cos u,  and\n   K(u) = −f''/f = cos u / (2 + cos u),\n   which is positive on the outer half and negative on the inner half. */\nload(dynamics)$\nftor(uu) := 2 + cos(uu)$\nKtor(uu) := cos(uu)/(2 + cos(uu))$\n\nprint(\"torus of revolution (tube 1, centre 2), K along a meridian:\")$\nfor uu in [0, %pi/4, %pi/2, 3*%pi/4, %pi] do\n  print(\"   u = \", uu, \"   K = \", ratsimp(Ktor(uu)),\n        \" ≈ \", float(Ktor(uu)))$\nprint(\"   K_max = 1/3 at the outer equator, K_min = −1 at the inner one.\")$\n\n/* Integrate the fan Jacobi field j'' + K j = 0, j(0)=0, j'(0)=1,\n   starting at the outer equator where curvature is most positive. */\nsol : rk([w, -Ktor(uv)*jv], [jv, w], [0, 1], [uv, 0, 2*%pi, 0.005])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"fan Jacobi field from the outer equator:\")$\nfor target in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 6.28] do block([row],\n  row : first(sublist(sol, lambda([p], p[1] >= target))),\n  print(\"   u = \", target, \"   j = \", float(row[2])) )$\njmin : lreduce(min, map(lambda([p], p[2]), sublist(sol, lambda([p], p[1] > 0.5))))$\nprint(\"   minimum of j over u ∈ (0.5, 2π] : \", float(jmin))$\nprint(\"   j never returns to zero: NO conjugate point along the meridian.\")$\n\n/* Why: reduction of order.  f is one Jacobi field (cell above), so the\n   independent one is  j = 3 f(u) ∫₀^u dt/f(t)², manifestly positive. */\nIres : quad_qags( 1/ftor(s)^2, s, 0, 2*%pi )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   closed form  j(u) = 3 f(u) ∫₀^u dt/f(t)²  (reduction of order):\")$\nprint(\"   ∫₀^{2π} dt/f² = \", Ires[1])$\nprint(\"   j(2π) = 3·f(2π)·that = \", 3*ftor(2*%pi)*Ires[1],\n      \"   vs numeric \", float(last(sol)[2]))$\nprint(\"   f > 0 and the integral is strictly increasing, so j > 0 for u > 0.\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"STURM COMPARISON.  K ≤ 1/3 everywhere, so no conjugate point can\")$\nprint(\"occur before that of the sphere with K = 1/3, namely\")$\nprint(\"   π/√K = \", float(%pi/sqrt(1/3)), \" = π√3.\")$\nprint(\"The meridian is a closed geodesic of length 2π = \", float(2*%pi),\n      \", which is LONGER\")$\nprint(\"than that bound — so comparison alone leaves room for a conjugate\")$\nprint(\"point somewhere in (π√3, 2π].  None appears.  The negative curvature\")$\nprint(\"on the inner half defocuses the geodesics enough that they never\")$\nprint(\"meet at all: a patch of positive curvature is not sufficient, and\")$\nprint(\"the comparison bound is only a lower bound, not a prediction.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "torus of revolution (tube 1, centre 2), K along a meridian:\n   u =  0    K =  1/3  ≈  0.3333333333333333\n   u =  %pi/4    K =  sqrt(2)/(sqrt(2)+4)  ≈  0.2612038749637414\n   u =  %pi/2    K =  0  ≈  0.0\n   u =  (3*%pi)/4    K =  sqrt(2)/(sqrt(2)-4)  ≈  -0.5469181606780271\n   u =  %pi    K =  -1  ≈  -1.0\n   K_max = 1/3 at the outer equator, K_min = −1 at the inner one.\nfan Jacobi field from the outer equator:\n   u =  1    j =  0.9509551106639175\n   u =  2    j =  1.7694801534787443\n   u =  3    j =  3.2377116992043327\n   u =  4    j =  7.724883677121417\n   u =  5    j =  15.37987242414313\n   u =  6    j =  21.19468447348524\n   u =  6.28    j =  21.762370259120406\n   minimum of j over u ∈ (0.5, 2π] :  0.49805820989446614\n   j never returns to zero: NO conjugate point along the meridian.\n   closed form  j(u) = 3 f(u) ∫₀^u dt/f(t)²  (reduction of order):\n   ∫₀^{2π} dt/f² =  2.4183991523122907\n   j(2π) = 3·f(2π)·that =  21.765592370810616    vs numeric\n                          21.765592370749715\n   f > 0 and the integral is strictly increasing, so j > 0 for u > 0.\nSTURM COMPARISON.  K ≤ 1/3 everywhere, so no conjugate point can\noccur before that of the sphere with K = 1/3, namely\n   π/√K =  5.441398092702653  = π√3.\nThe meridian is a closed geodesic of length 2π =\n  6.283185307179586 , which is LONGER\nthan that bound — so comparison alone leaves room for a conjugate\npoint somewhere in (π√3, 2π].  None appears.  The negative curvature\non the inner half defocuses the geodesics enough that they never\nmeet at all: a patch of positive curvature is not sufficient, and\nthe comparison bound is only a lower bound, not a prediction."
     ]
    }
   ]
