  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Geodesic polar coordinates and the three model behaviours --- */\n/* g = dρ² + s_K(ρ)² dθ².  s_K is the fan Jacobi field, and it is also the\n   Jacobian factor of exp_p — so exp is singular exactly where s_K = 0. */\nsK(Kv, rho) := if Kv > 0 then sin(sqrt(Kv)*rho)/sqrt(Kv)\n               elseif Kv = 0 then rho\n               else sinh(sqrt(-Kv)*rho)/sqrt(-Kv)$\n\nprint(\"     ρ        s_1(ρ)      s_0(ρ)      s_-1(ρ)\")$\nfor rr in [0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.141592653589793, 4.0] do\n  print(\"   \", rr, \"   \", float(sK(1,rr)), \"   \", float(sK(0,rr)),\n        \"   \", float(sK(-1,rr)))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"s_1 hits zero at ρ = π: exp_p is singular there, the whole sphere of\")$\nprint(\"directions is focused onto the antipode, and geodesics stop minimising.\")$\nprint(\"s_0 and s_-1 never return to zero: exp_p is a diffeomorphism onto its\")$\nprint(\"image for all ρ.  That is CARTAN–HADAMARD in its simplest instance —\")$\nprint(\"complete + simply connected + K ≤ 0 ⟹ M ≅ ℝⁿ.\")$\n\n/* Areas of geodesic discs — Bishop–Gromov in dimension 2 */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"area of a geodesic disc of radius ρ (2π ∫₀^ρ s_K):\")$\nprint(\"     ρ       K = 1        K = 0        K = -1\")$\nfor rr in [0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0] do\n  print(\"   \", rr, \"   \", float(2*%pi*(1-cos(rr))), \"   \", float(%pi*rr^2),\n        \"   \", float(2*%pi*(cosh(rr)-1)))$\nprint(\"   ordered K=1 < K=0 < K=-1 at every radius, as Bishop–Gromov requires.\")$\n\n/* --- Bonnet–Myers, with numbers --- */\n/* The diameter of S²(R₀) is πR₀ — the pole-to-pole distance, half a great\n   circle — not the circumference 2πR₀. */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"BONNET–MYERS:  Ric ≥ (n−1)K g,  K > 0  ⟹  diam ≤ π/√K.\")$\nprint(\"   space          Ric bound K      π/√K            diam\")$\nfor R0v in [1, 2, 3] do\n  print(\"   S²(R₀=\", R0v, \")        \", float(1/R0v^2), \"     \",\n        float(%pi/sqrt(1/R0v^2)), \"     \", float(%pi*R0v))$\nprint(\"   equality in every row: the round sphere is the extremal case,\")$\nprint(\"   which is what makes the theorem sharp.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   ℝP² = S²(1)/± :  K = 1, bound π = \", float(%pi),\n      \", diam = π/2 = \", float(%pi/2))$\nprint(\"      strictly inside the bound; π₁ = ℤ/2, finite ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   flat torus :  Ric = 0, so Ric ≥ (n−1)K g fails for every K > 0.\")$\nprint(\"      No bound applies — and indeed π₁ = ℤ² is infinite, even though\")$\nprint(\"      the torus is compact.  Ric ≥ 0 is not enough; the theorem needs\")$\nprint(\"      a strictly positive lower bound, and the flat torus is exactly\")$\nprint(\"      the example that shows why.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
