     "text": [
      "     ρ        s_1(ρ)      s_0(ρ)      s_-1(ρ)\n    0.5     0.479425538604203     0.5     0.5210953054937474\n    1.0     0.8414709848078965     1.0     1.1752011936438014\n    2.0     0.9092974268256817     2.0     3.6268604078470186\n    3.0     0.1411200080598672     3.0     10.017874927409903\n    3.141592653589793     1.2246467991473532e-16     3.141592653589793\n   11.548739357257746\n    4.0     -0.7568024953079283     4.0     27.289917197127753\ns_1 hits zero at ρ = π: exp_p is singular there, the whole sphere of\ndirections is focused onto the antipode, and geodesics stop minimising.\ns_0 and s_-1 never return to zero: exp_p is a diffeomorphism onto its\nimage for all ρ.  That is CARTAN–HADAMARD in its simplest instance —\ncomplete + simply connected + K ≤ 0 ⟹ M ≅ ℝⁿ.\narea of a geodesic disc of radius ρ (2π ∫₀^ρ s_K):\n     ρ       K = 1        K = 0        K = -1\n    0.5     0.7691714484729762     0.7853981633974483     0.8018975893993443\n    1.0     2.88836579751364     3.141592653589793     3.412276265284902\n    2.0     8.897912996201855     12.566370614359172     17.355387381771436\n    3.0     12.503491616037541     28.274333882308138     56.97380062234158\n   ordered K=1 < K=0 < K=-1 at every radius, as Bishop–Gromov requires.\nBONNET–MYERS:  Ric ≥ (n−1)K g,  K > 0  ⟹  diam ≤ π/√K.\n   space          Ric bound K      π/√K            diam\n   S²(R₀= 1 )         1.0       3.141592653589793       3.141592653589793\n   S²(R₀= 2 )         0.25       6.283185307179586       6.283185307179586\n   S²(R₀= 3 )         0.1111111111111111       9.42477796076938\n         9.42477796076938\n   equality in every row: the round sphere is the extremal case,\n   which is what makes the theorem sharp.\n   ℝP² = S²(1)/± :  K = 1, bound π =  3.141592653589793 , diam = π/2 =\n                                     1.5707963267948966\n      strictly inside the bound; π₁ = ℤ/2, finite ✓\n   flat torus :  Ric = 0, so Ric ≥ (n−1)K g fails for every K > 0.\n      No bound applies — and indeed π₁ = ℤ² is infinite, even though\n      the torus is compact.  Ric ≥ 0 is not enough; the theorem needs\n      a strictly positive lower bound, and the flat torus is exactly\n      the example that shows why."
