   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\ddot j + K j = 0$$\n\n**The vector equation, verified.** On the unit sphere the meridian family gives $J = \\partial_v$ — constant *components*, but not a parallel field. Computing $D^2J/dt^2 = [0,-1]$ and $R(J,\\dot\\gamma)\\dot\\gamma = [0,1]$ from the Christoffel symbols directly, their sum is exactly zero. And $|J| = \\sin t$, which is the scalar fan solution for $K = 1$.\n\n**Surfaces of revolution give it away for free.** For $g = du^2 + f(u)^2dv^2$ the curvature is $K = -f''/f$, so $f'' + Kf = 0$ identically: **$f$ is itself a Jacobi field**, the variation field of the meridians. Conjugate points are then just the zeros of $f$ — $\\sin u$ has one at $\\pi$, $r$ and $\\sinh u$ have none.\n\n**Positive curvature in a patch is not enough.** The torus of revolution (tube 1, centre 2) has $K = \\cos u/(2+\\cos u)$: $+1/3$ at the outer equator, $-1$ at the inner. Integrating the fan field numerically along a meridian, $j$ climbs from 0 to about 21.8 over the full $2\\pi$ and never returns — no conjugate point. This was confirmed against the closed form from reduction of order, $j = 3f(u)\\int_0^u dt/f^2$, agreeing to ten digits.\n\nThe Sturm comparison bound says a conjugate point cannot appear before $\\pi/\\sqrt{1/3} = \\pi\\sqrt3 \\approx 5.44$; the meridian is $2\\pi \\approx 6.28$ long, so comparison *permits* one in the last stretch. None occurs. **A comparison theorem gives a lower bound, not a prediction** — the negative curvature inside defocuses more than enough to compensate.\n\n**Comparison theorems, with numbers.** For $S^2(R_0)$, Bonnet–Myers gives $\\mathrm{diam} \\le \\pi/\\sqrt{K} = \\pi R_0$, and the actual diameter is $\\pi R_0$: equality in every row, so the theorem is sharp with the round sphere as extremal case. $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ sits strictly inside the bound with $\\pi_1 = \\mathbb{Z}/2$.\n\nThe flat torus is the instructive failure. It is compact with $\\mathrm{Ric} = 0$, so it satisfies $\\mathrm{Ric} \\ge 0$ but not $\\mathrm{Ric} \\ge (n-1)Kg$ for any $K > 0$ — and its $\\pi_1 = \\mathbb{Z}^2$ is infinite. The strict positivity in the hypothesis is doing real work.\n\n**Cartan–Hadamard in one line of output.** In geodesic polar coordinates $g = d\\rho^2 + s_K(\\rho)^2d\\theta^2$, the function $s_K$ is both the Jacobi field and the Jacobian of $\\exp_p$. $s_1$ vanishes at $\\rho = \\pi$; $s_0 = \\rho$ and $s_{-1} = \\sinh\\rho$ never return to zero. No conjugate points, so $\\exp_p$ is a diffeomorphism and the space is $\\mathbb{R}^n$ topologically. The disc areas came out ordered $K{=}1 < K{=}0 < K{=}{-1}$ at every radius, which is Bishop–Gromov in dimension 2.\n\n## Next\n\n**4.07 — Space Forms and Constant Curvature** closes Stage 4 by classifying the spaces where $K$ is the same in every direction at every point. There are exactly three simply connected models — $S^n$, $\\mathbb{E}^n$, $H^n$ — which are the three geometries Stage 2 built by hand from projective and Möbius considerations. Everything else is a quotient.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 10 (Jacobi fields, conjugate points, the second variation) and Chapter 11 (comparison theory, Bonnet–Myers, Cartan–Hadamard).\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1992) *Riemannian Geometry*. Boston: Birkhäuser. Chapters 5 and 7, and Chapter 9 for the Rauch comparison theorem in its general form.\n- Cheeger, J. and Ebin, D.G. (2008) *Comparison Theorems in Riemannian Geometry*. Providence: AMS Chelsea. The dedicated treatment, if comparison geometry is where you want to go.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Geodesic deviation as tidal acceleration — the Jacobi equation under its physical name.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. The torus result — no conjugate point despite a region of positive curvature — was obtained numerically and then confirmed against an independent closed form, not assumed.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
